WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.232
Missing values require reasoning. There is no single correct command that works for every situation.

00:00:06.232 --> 00:00:10.637
In this example, the city column contains one missing value.

00:00:10.637 --> 00:00:13.782
We replace that value with the label Unknown.

00:00:13.782 --> 00:00:17.932
For amount, we demonstrate filling the missing value with the median.

00:00:17.932 --> 00:00:21.782
Click Run and compare the missing-value counts before and after.

00:00:21.782 --> 00:00:25.727
The important lesson is not that median is always the correct answer.

00:00:25.727 --> 00:00:27.897
It is only a teaching example.

00:00:27.897 --> 00:00:32.162
Imagine that amount is missing because the transaction was cancelled.

00:00:32.162 --> 00:00:36.717
Filling it with the median might create a false transaction value.

00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:42.692
In another dataset, a missing city may mean the customer refused to provide a location.

00:00:42.692 --> 00:00:48.592
Replacing it with Unknown may be appropriate because the missingness itself has meaning.

00:00:48.592 --> 00:00:53.137
Other choices include dropping the row, leaving the value missing, using a business rule,

00:00:53.137 --> 00:00:59.222
or creating a separate indicator showing that the original value was missing.

00:00:59.222 --> 00:01:03.273
Always ask why the value is missing before deciding how to handle it.
